"# Appendix D — The Cortex Memory Verb Reference

This is the flat reference for Motoi's memory verbs — every wired verb of the Cortex, each with its signature, what it returns, and a runnable example you can trust. The narrative chapters taught when and why to use these; this appendix is the lookup you turn back to when you just need the shape. There is no `cortex/remind` verb; a reminder is `cortex/remember` now and `cortex/recall` later. Every fence below runs.

The verbs split cleanly into four groups: **store** (write, remember, record), **retrieve** (recall, read, query, topk, keys, size, search), **evict** (forget, prune), and **graph** (associate, lookup). Each entry states the signature, the return value, what the verb is for, and shows it working. Edge cases and contrasts follow the main entries.

## Store

### `cortex/write`

**Signature:** `(cortex/write key value)` → `#t`. The key-value door. Upserts: writing a key that already exists replaces its value, so a key holds exactly one current value. `key` is a string; `value` is any Motoi value. Salience is always 0.5 — to set salience, use `cortex/remember` instead.

```scheme
;; write stores a value under a key and returns #t. Upsert: the last write wins.
(cortex/write \"ref-d/write-demo\" \"stored by write\")
```

Returns `#t`. Read it back with `cortex/read` under the same key.

**When to reach for `write`:** When you have a single named value and no opinion about salience. The fastest door into the store. Use `remember` if the record needs a keep-weight.

**Upsert behavior:** A second `write` to the same key silently replaces the first value. There is no error, no duplicate — just the new value in the same slot.

```scheme
;; Demonstrate upsert: second write wins, first is gone.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/upsert-demo\" \"first\")
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/upsert-demo\" \"second\")
  (cortex/read \"ref-d/upsert-demo\"))
```

Returns `\"second\"` — the first value was silently replaced.

### `cortex/remember`

**Signature:** `(cortex/remember key value [:salience s] [:tags (...)])` → `#t`. Like `write`, but accepts extra fields — most importantly `:salience`, a keep-weight from 0 to 1 (default 0.5) that governs whether the record survives a prune. Also upserts by key. Tags are a list of labels that can group records thematically.

```scheme
;; remember stores with an optional salience — a high keep-weight resists pruning.
(cortex/remember \"ref-d/remember-demo\" \"stored by remember\" :salience 0.9)
```

Returns `#t`. The record now carries a salience of 0.9, marking it as something to hold tightly.

**When to reach for `remember`:** Whenever the record has a salience opinion — rules, lessons, reminders, boundary notes, preferences. High salience (0.8–0.95) for the keepers; low (0.1–0.3) for scratch notes you expect to prune.

**Re-remembering as upsert:** Calling `cortex/remember` on a key that already exists replaces the record with the new value and salience. This is how you bump a salience mid-session: `(cortex/remember \"k\" \"same value\" :salience 0.85)` raises the key from whatever salience it had before.

```scheme
;; Bump salience: re-remember the same key at a higher keep-weight.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"ref-d/bump-demo\" \"a note\" :salience 0.3)
  (cortex/remember \"ref-d/bump-demo\" \"a note\" :salience 0.85)
  (pair? (cortex/recall \"ref-d/bump-demo\")))
```

Returns `#t` — the key was re-remembered at the higher salience; the record is there.

### `cortex/record`

**Signature:** `(cortex/record :field value ...)` → `#t`. The free-form door. Appends a structured memory record built from the keyword fields you pass — no key required. Use it for a small event with several parts. Retrieve these by content with `cortex/query`, not by key.

```scheme
;; record appends a free-form, multi-field memory and returns #t. No key needed.
(cortex/record :text \"a worked drill\" :topic \"folding\" :grade \"pass\")
```

Returns `#t`. A structured record with `:text`, `:topic`, and `:grade` now lives in the store.

**When to reach for `record`:** For events — a debugging session, a conversation turn, an error trace, a test result — anything with multiple named parts. The fields you choose become the queryable surface.

**Field naming:** Any keyword can be a field. Common conventions: `:text` for the human-readable description, `:kind` for the category (`:error`, `:episode`, `:correction`, `:session-note`), `:verb` for the verb involved, `:topic` for the subject area. The store imposes no schema.

```scheme
;; A richer record: kind, text, and several contextual fields.
(begin
  (cortex/record
    :kind :correction
    :text \"reached for fold-left; correct verb is fold\"
    :verb-wrong \"fold-left\"
    :verb-right \"fold\"
    :reason \"R6RS excluded from Motoi\")
  (>= (length (cortex/query :kind :correction)) 1))
```

Returns `#t` — the structured correction is stored and queryable by kind.

## Retrieve

### `cortex/recall`

**Signature:** `(cortex/recall key)` → record alist, or `#f`. Looks up a key and returns the *whole record* as an association list — key, text, salience, timestamp, provenance. Returns `#f` when the key was never stored: an honest null you can branch on. Searches the knowledge tier if nothing is found in memory.

```scheme
;; recall returns the whole record (an alist), or an honest #f if the key is unknown.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"ref-d/recall-demo\" \"recall me\")
  (list (pair? (cortex/recall \"ref-d/recall-demo\"))
        (cortex/recall \"ref-d/never-stored-xyz\")))
```

Returns `(#t #f)` — a real record comes back as a pair; a missing key comes back `#f`.

**When to reach for `recall`:** When you want the full record with its metadata — the salience, the timestamp, the provenance. If you only need the value, `read` is lighter. If you need to know when something was written, or how salient it is, `recall` gives you the envelope.

### `cortex/read`

**Signature:** `(cortex/read key)` → the bare value, or `#f`. Like `recall`, but returns just the stored value (the string or the data), not the wrapping record. Use `read` when you want the thing; use `recall` when you want its metadata too. Honest `#f` on a miss.

```scheme
;; read returns the bare stored value, or #f when the key isn't there.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/read-demo\" \"just the value\")
  (list (cortex/read \"ref-d/read-demo\")
        (cortex/read \"ref-d/absent-key-xyz\")))
```

Returns `(\"just the value\" #f)` — the value itself, and an honest null for the absent key.

**When to reach for `read`:** Most of the time. When you know the key and you want what's behind it, `read` is the right door — lighter than `recall`, returns the value directly.

**The `read`/`recall` contrast:** They share the same lookup semantics. The difference is the return type: `read` strips the envelope and returns the payload; `recall` includes the envelope. If the record is a string, `read` returns the string; `recall` returns an alist where one field holds the same string plus the salience, timestamp, and provenance alongside.

### `cortex/query`

**Signature:** `(cortex/query :field value ...)` → list of matching records. Searches the structured records (those from `cortex/record`) for those whose data matches *every* field you give, and returns them all as a list. Content search, not a key lookup.

```scheme
;; query returns every structured record whose data matches all given fields.
(begin
  (cortex/record :text \"drill one\" :topic \"ref-d-query\" :grade \"pass\")
  (cortex/record :text \"drill two\" :topic \"ref-d-query\" :grade \"pass\")
  (>= (length (cortex/query :topic \"ref-d-query\" :grade \"pass\")) 2))
```

Returns `#t` — at least the two records we appended matched the filter.

**When to reach for `query`:** When you wrote the records with `record` and now want to find them by their fields rather than by a key. The fields you passed to `record` are what `query` matches against. Multi-field queries narrow the result: `(cortex/query :kind :error :verb \"fold-left\")` returns only error records about fold-left.

### `cortex/topk`

**Signature:** `(cortex/topk k [:field value ...])` → list of the `k` most-recent records, newest first. An optional filter narrows it to matching structured records. The recency window on memory. Ranked by timestamp, not by salience — it is a recency view, not a salience ranking.

```scheme
;; topk returns the k most-recently-stored records, newest first.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/topk-a\" 1)
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/topk-b\" 2)
  (length (cortex/topk 2)))
```

Returns `2` — the two freshest records handed back as a list.

**When to reach for `topk`:** When you want a window on recent memory — what have I been working on lately, what are the last few conversation turns. The `k` is a soft cap on the window size; give it a number proportional to how far back you want to look.

**topk with a filter:** Pass `:field value` pairs after the count to narrow the window to matching records. `(cortex/topk 10 :kind :episode)` gives the 10 most recent episode records, for example.

```scheme
;; topk with a filter: only the most recent records of a specific kind.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :ref-d-note :text \"first note\")
  (cortex/record :kind :ref-d-note :text \"second note\")
  (>= (length (cortex/topk 5 :kind :ref-d-note)) 1))
```

Returns `#t` — at least one `:ref-d-note` record was returned in the top-5 window.

### `cortex/keys`

**Signature:** `(cortex/keys)` → list of all memory keys. The table of contents. Read-only; changes nothing.

```scheme
;; keys returns every stored memory key — the table of contents.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/keys-demo\" \"here\")
  (list (list? (cortex/keys))
        (if (member \"ref-d/keys-demo\" (cortex/keys)) #t #f)))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — a real list, and it contains the key we just wrote.

**When to reach for `keys`:** When you want to browse what's in the store — a sanity check before a major prune, or to find the name of a key you half-remember. `keys` is the index; it doesn't retrieve the values, only their names.

### `cortex/size`

**Signature:** `(cortex/size)` → number of stored records. The fill gauge. Watch it climb toward a cap; that's the signal to prune. Counts only the memory tier (episodic records); knowledge-tier records are virtual and excluded from the count.

```scheme
;; size returns the record count — the gauge you watch against a cap.
(let ((before (cortex/size)))
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/size-demo\" \"one more\")
  (> (cortex/size) 0))
```

Returns `#t` — the store holds records, and it grew by the one we added.

**When to reach for `size`:** Before and after any significant write activity, to check whether a prune is warranted. The pattern is: `(when (> (cortex/size) cap) (cortex/prune :min-salience floor))`.

### `cortex/search`

**Signature:** `(cortex/search query [k])` → list of up to `k` matching records. Token-overlap RAG search across both the memory tier and the knowledge tier. Default `k=8`. Returns records from both tiers, each tagged with `:kind :memory` or `:kind :knowledge`. The widest-net retrieval verb.

```scheme
;; search crosses both the memory tier and the knowledge tier in one call.
(let ((hits (cortex/search \"fold\" 5)))
  (list? hits))
```

Returns `#t` — a list of records, potentially mixing memory and knowledge entries.

**When to reach for `search`:** When you don't know the key, don't know the field structure, and only know the topic. Also useful as a fallback after a `recall` returns `#f`: if the exact key isn't known, search by topic and check the hits.

## Evict

### `cortex/forget`

**Signature:** `(cortex/forget key)` → `#t` if a record was removed, `#f` if the key wasn't there. The scalpel: removes exactly one record by name, regardless of salience.

```scheme
;; forget drops one record by key: #t if found and removed, #f if it wasn't there.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/forget-demo\" \"soon gone\")
  (list (cortex/forget \"ref-d/forget-demo\")
        (cortex/forget \"ref-d/was-never-here-xyz\")))
```

Returns `(#t #f)` — the first found its record and removed it; the second honestly reports nothing to forget.

**When to reach for `forget`:** When a specific key has become stale or has done its job — a reminder that was recalled and acted on, a temporary session flag that's no longer relevant, a superseded rule. `forget` is surgical; it touches exactly one record.

**`forget` versus `prune`:** `forget` removes one record by name; `prune` removes many records by a salience floor or age horizon. Use `forget` when you know *which* record to remove; use `prune` when you want to sweep a class of records.

### `cortex/prune`

**Signature:** `(cortex/prune [:min-salience s] [:older-than ms] [:keep n])` → count evicted. The sweep: with `:min-salience s` it drops every record whose salience is *below* `s` and returns how many left. `:older-than ms` drops records older than `ms` milliseconds; `:keep n` keeps only the `n` most recent. With no arguments, it drops the low scratch (salience below 0.1). The salience floor is the primary eviction axis.

```scheme
;; prune drops records below the salience floor and returns the count evicted.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"ref-d/prune-low\" \"scratch\" :salience 0.1)
  (cortex/remember \"ref-d/prune-high\" \"keeper\" :salience 0.9)
  (let ((evicted (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.3)))
    (list (>= evicted 1)                       ; at least the 0.1 record left
          (cortex/read \"ref-d/prune-low\")     ; evicted -> #f
          (string? (cortex/read \"ref-d/prune-high\"))))) ; kept -> string
```

Returns a list like `(#t #f #t)` — the low-salience record fell below the 0.3 floor and was evicted (now `#f`), while the high-salience keeper survived. That is eviction by judgement: you set the floor, salience decides who stays.

**Three prune axes:**

- `:min-salience s` — drop everything with salience below `s`. The primary axis. Run the model's judgement first (bump survivors), then apply this floor.
- `:older-than ms` — drop everything written more than `ms` milliseconds ago. Independent of salience; sweeps by age.
- `:keep n` — keep the `n` most-recent records (recency-ranked), evict everything older. The hard cap enforcer.

The axes compose: `(cortex/prune :min-salience 0.2 :older-than 3600000)` evicts any record that is *either* below the salience floor *or* more than one hour old.

```scheme
;; Layered prune: floor then age then hard cap — three sweeps, one motion.
(let* ((a (cortex/prune :min-salience 0.1))
       (b (cortex/prune :older-than 86400000))
       (c (cortex/prune :keep 300)))
  (list (>= a 0) (>= b 0) (>= c 0)))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — all three axes ran cleanly.

## Graph

### `cortex/associate`

**Signature:** `(cortex/associate from to label)` → `#t`. Appends a labeled directed edge from the key `from` to the key `to`. The edge is stored in `cortex.slatl` as a `cortex-edge` form alongside the records. Labels are symbols you invent.

```scheme
;; associate draws a labeled directed edge from one key to another.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/node-a\" \"the source node\")
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/node-b\" \"the target node\")
  (cortex/associate \"ref-d/node-a\" \"ref-d/node-b\" :points-to))
```

Returns `#t`. A `:points-to` edge from `node-a` to `node-b` is now in the store.

**When to reach for `associate`:** When two records are related and you want to navigate between them. Common labels: `:explains` (rule explains fact), `:follows` (conversation turn follows prior), `:corrects` (reminder corrects error record), `:applies-to` (rule applies to specific verb), `:related` (general-purpose link).

**Multiple edges:** You can draw multiple labeled edges from the same source key. Each `associate` call appends a new edge; they don't overwrite each other.

```scheme
;; Multiple edges from one key: two different relationships.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/hub\" \"a central concept\")
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/spoke-x\" \"one detail\")
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/spoke-y\" \"another detail\")
  (cortex/associate \"ref-d/hub\" \"ref-d/spoke-x\" :details)
  (cortex/associate \"ref-d/hub\" \"ref-d/spoke-y\" :details)
  (>= (length (cortex/lookup \"ref-d/hub\" :details)) 2))
```

Returns `#t` — both `:details` edges are present and lookup finds both targets.

### `cortex/lookup`

**Signature:** `(cortex/lookup key [label])` → list of target keys, or `#f`. Walks edges out of `key`. With a label, returns only edges with that label. Without a label, returns all edge targets. Returns an empty list when edges exist but none match the label; returns `#f` when the key itself is unknown.

```scheme
;; lookup walks edges from a source key — returns the list of targets.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/lookup-src\" \"the source\")
  (cortex/write \"ref-d/lookup-tgt\" \"the target\")
  (cortex/associate \"ref-d/lookup-src\" \"ref-d/lookup-tgt\" :leads-to)
  (cortex/lookup \"ref-d/lookup-src\" :leads-to))
```

Returns `(\"ref-d/lookup-tgt\")` — the edge was drawn and lookup followed it.

**When to reach for `lookup`:** When you have a concept and want to navigate to related records by following a labeled relationship. Build the graph with `associate`; navigate it with `lookup` plus `read`/`recall` to fetch the actual records.

## The whole vocabulary at a glance

Three to store, seven to retrieve, two to evict, two for the graph. Store with `write` (plain key-value, salience 0.5), `remember` (key-value with salience + tags), or `record` (free-form structured event). Retrieve with `read` (bare value by key), `recall` (whole record by key), `query` (records by field), `topk` (recency window), `keys` (all names), `size` (the count), or `search` (topic query across tiers). Evict with `forget` (one by name) or `prune` (a salience/age/cap sweep). Graph with `associate` (draw an edge) and `lookup` (walk edges). Every retrieve verb answers an honest `#f` or empty when it has nothing — the null is signal, not a gap. And there is no reminder verb: to remind, remember now and recall later. That is the entire memory of the language, and it fits in a paragraph on purpose.

## Quick decision guide

*What verb, for what job?*

- **One named value to store:** `cortex/write`.
- **One named value with a keep-weight:** `cortex/remember` with `:salience`.
- **A structured event with many parts:** `cortex/record` with keyword fields.
- **Get the value for a key I know:** `cortex/read`.
- **Get the full record (salience, timestamp) for a key:** `cortex/recall`.
- **Find records by their field contents:** `cortex/query`.
- **See what's been written most recently:** `cortex/topk k`.
- **Find anything touching a topic, across all tiers:** `cortex/search`.
- **Browse the table of contents:** `cortex/keys`.
- **Check how full the store is:** `cortex/size`.
- **Remove one specific key:** `cortex/forget`.
- **Sweep records below a salience floor:** `cortex/prune :min-salience s`.
- **Enforce a hard cap on total records:** `cortex/prune :keep n`.
- **Drop records older than a horizon:** `cortex/prune :older-than ms`.
- **Connect two records by a relationship:** `cortex/associate from to label`.
- **Walk relationships out of a record:** `cortex/lookup key label`.

## Extended worked examples

### A cross-session grounding workflow

The practical test of the memory verbs is whether they work together across distinct goals. Here is a complete cross-session grounding workflow — the kind you'd run the first time you encounter a new SRFI verb, so that all future sessions can skip the grounding step.

The goal: learn that `string-contains` is from SRFI-13, understand its return type (an integer index or `#f`, not a boolean), file a reminder about the return type, and wire the grounding note to the reminder so both are navigable from a single hub.

```scheme
;; Cross-session grounding: learn a verb, file a reminder, wire them.
(begin
  ;; 1. Ground the verb — what it is and where it comes from.
  (cortex/remember \"grounded/string-contains\"
    \"SRFI-13. Returns the start index of the first match, or #f. NOT a boolean.\"
    :salience 0.85)
  ;; 2. File a reminder about the return-type subtlety that trips people.
  (cortex/remember \"remind/string-contains-returns-index\"
    \"string-contains returns an INTEGER (the start index), not #t. Index 0 is truthy.\"
    :salience 0.82)
  ;; 3. Wire the reminder to the grounding so they are navigable together.
  (cortex/associate \"grounded/string-contains\" \"remind/string-contains-returns-index\" :flags)
  ;; 4. Verify: from the grounding, walk to the reminder.
  (let ((links (cortex/lookup \"grounded/string-contains\" :flags)))
    (list (string? (cortex/read \"grounded/string-contains\"))
          (>= (length links) 1)
          (string? (cortex/read (car links))))))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — the grounding note is stored, the `:flags` edge exists, and the reminder at the end of the edge is also readable. Three verbs working as one system: `remember` to write, `associate` to link, `lookup` to navigate. A future session that encounters `string-contains` starts from the grounding, walks one edge, and has the return-type reminder in hand without re-deriving it.

### Using `cortex/topk` with a filter to scope recent work

`cortex/topk` with a `:kind` filter gives you a recency window scoped to a specific record type. This is useful when you want to review recent corrections without also seeing recent grounding notes or boundary records — the filter narrows to just the category you care about.

```scheme
;; Seed several typed records and pull the most recent corrections specifically.
(begin
  (cortex/record :kind :correction :text \"string-concat→string-append\" :salience 0.75)
  (cortex/record :kind :correction :text \"mapcar→map\" :salience 0.75)
  (cortex/record :kind :grounding   :text \"string-upcase is SRFI-13\" :salience 0.8)
  ;; Only the corrections — most recent first, limited to 5.
  (let ((recent-corrections (cortex/topk 5 :kind :correction)))
    (>= (length recent-corrections) 2)))
```

Returns `#t` — at least the two corrections we appended came back in the window. The grounding record was excluded because its kind doesn't match. Typed topk lets you build a short dashboard of recent activity per category without sifting through a mixed list.

### A prune-size-verify loop with a hard cap

Here is the full sequence that confirms the hard cap actually brings the store back under a limit: write enough records to exceed a soft threshold, then apply the hard-cap prune, then verify size dropped.

```scheme
;; Hard-cap loop: flood, cap, verify under threshold.
(begin
  ;; Write several records — some low-salience, some mid.
  (cortex/remember \"hc/a\" \"record one\"   :salience 0.3)
  (cortex/remember \"hc/b\" \"record two\"   :salience 0.3)
  (cortex/remember \"hc/c\" \"record three\" :salience 0.6)
  (cortex/remember \"hc/d\" \"record four\"  :salience 0.6)
  (cortex/remember \"hc/e\" \"record five\"  :salience 0.9)
  (let ((before-cap (cortex/size)))
    ;; Apply hard cap at a large number — this evicts nothing if we're under.
    (let ((swept (cortex/prune :keep 500)))
      ;; Verify the cap ran without error and the size is still sensible.
      (list (>= before-cap 5)
            (>= swept 0)
            (> (cortex/size) 0)))))
```

Returns `(#t #t #t)` — the store had at least five records before the cap, the prune ran cleanly, and the store is non-empty afterward. In a session where you're actually over 500 records, the cap would evict the oldest; here it's a clean no-op. The pattern transfers to any cap value: use `:keep n` as the last resort after salience and age sweeps have done their work.

### A session that archives old notes into a summary record

A long session accumulates many individual notes. Before pruning, a useful pattern is to consolidate the low-salience ones into a single summary record and then sweep the originals. The summary captures the gist; the originals are released. This is memory hygiene with intent.

```scheme
;; Consolidate several low-salience notes into a summary, then release them.
(begin
  ;; Three temporary working notes from earlier in the session.
  (cortex/remember \"temp/note-fold\"    \"tried fold with wrong acc order\" :salience 0.25)
  (cortex/remember \"temp/note-filter\"  \"filter predicate was backwards\" :salience 0.25)
  (cortex/remember \"temp/note-strings\" \"used string-concat, should be string-append\" :salience 0.25)
  ;; Archive the gist into a single higher-salience summary.
  (cortex/remember \"archive/session-errors-20260730\"
    \"Errors today: fold acc-order, filter predicate, string-concat→string-append.\"
    :salience 0.7)
  ;; Release the individual notes.
  (cortex/forget \"temp/note-fold\")
  (cortex/forget \"temp/note-filter\")
  (cortex/forget \"temp/note-strings\")
  ;; Confirm: summary is present; originals are gone.
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"archive/session-errors-20260730\"))
        (cortex/read \"temp/note-fold\")
        (cortex/read \"temp/note-filter\")
        (cortex/read \"temp/note-strings\")))
```

Returns `(#t #f #f #f)` — the summary record is present; the three originals are gone. The store carries the essential information (three error types) in one record at a useful salience, instead of three records at a low salience that would eventually be swept anyway. The archive pattern compresses while preserving; the sweep pattern discards. Use archive when the gist matters; sweep when even the gist doesn't.

### Reading provenance to distinguish your notes from seed records

In a well-managed store, records carry a `:provenance` field that tells you where they came from. Seed records are tagged with `\"local-memory.seed\"`; records you write don't carry a provenance unless you add one. Reading provenance lets you quickly distinguish between the fixed foundation and your session's contribution.

```scheme
;; Write a record with provenance, then use recall to inspect it.
(begin
  (cortex/record :key \"prov/my-finding\" :text \"fold is SRFI-1\" :provenance \"session-2026-07-30\")
  ;; recall returns the full record including any provenance field you attached
  (let ((r (cortex/recall \"prov/my-finding\")))
    (if r \"provenance-record-found\" \"not-found\")))
```

Returns `\"provenance-record-found\"` — the full record, including provenance, came back via `recall`. In a real session you'd check `(assoc :provenance r)` to read the provenance value and confirm whether this is your note or something from the seed. That distinction matters when you're auditing the store: seed records are invariants; your records are the accumulation of the session's work.

## Common patterns and anti-patterns

### Pattern: guard-with-recall before an action

The most common production pattern is checking memory before acting, so that a reminder or prior finding steers the action without needing to re-derive anything. `cortex/recall` returns the whole record (or `#f`), so the guard is a one-liner.

```scheme
;; Guard pattern: check memory before the action, follow the reminder if present.
(begin
  (cortex/remember \"guard-demo/fold-is-element-first\"
    \"SRFI-1 fold: element before accumulator in the combining function.\"
    :salience 0.85)
  (let ((guard (cortex/recall \"guard-demo/fold-is-element-first\")))
    (if guard
        ;; guided: use the correct element-first shape
        (fold (lambda (elem acc) (+ acc (* elem elem))) 0 (list 1 2 3 4))
        ;; unguided: re-derive from scratch
        \"no guard — re-deriving\")))
```

Returns `30` — 1+4+9+16, computed with the element-first combining function that the guard confirmed. The sum-of-squares is correct because the reminder kept the argument order straight.

### Pattern: topk as a session summary

At any point during a session you can pull a recency window and treat it as a summary of what's been happening. This is useful before a significant action (like a prune sweep) to confirm what's in the recent record.

```scheme
;; Session summary: pull the recent window to review before a prune.
(begin
  (cortex/write \"summary/verb-a\" \"ground note 1\")
  (cortex/write \"summary/verb-b\" \"ground note 2\")
  (cortex/write \"summary/lesson\" \"a lesson from this session\")
  ;; Check the three most recent records before sweeping.
  (let ((recent (cortex/topk 3)))
    (list (= (length recent) 3)
          (list? recent))))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — three records in the recency window, returned as a proper list. In practice you'd walk this list to check which records are low-salience candidates for the upcoming prune and bump any that deserve protection.

### Anti-pattern: using `cortex/write` when salience matters

`cortex/write` is always salience 0.5. If you're writing a lesson that needs to survive a squeeze, always use `cortex/remember` with an explicit `:salience`. The anti-pattern is filing a boundary fact or a reminder with `cortex/write` and then losing it when the next aggressive prune sweeps at 0.6.

```scheme
;; Anti-pattern (shown as a contrast): write gives 0.5; remember gives you control.
(begin
  ;; This fact will be swept if you prune at :min-salience 0.6 — that's the risk.
  (cortex/write \"anti-pattern/at-risk\" \"filed at default 0.5 — not safe above 0.5 floor\")
  ;; The safe form: explicit salience communicates intent to the store.
  (cortex/remember \"anti-pattern/safe\" \"filed at 0.85 — safe through any normal prune\" :salience 0.85)
  (list (string? (cortex/read \"anti-pattern/at-risk\"))
        (string? (cortex/read \"anti-pattern/safe\"))))
```

Returns `(#t #t)` — both are readable right now. The distinction becomes visible only when you prune at 0.6: the first record would be swept; the second would survive. The anti-pattern costs you a lesson exactly when the store comes under pressure — the worst possible time to lose it.

### Anti-pattern: forgetting `cortex/topk` is recency-ranked, not salience-ranked

A common mental model error: calling `cortex/topk 5` expecting the five most-*important* records. `topk` gives the five most-*recent*. For importance-ranked retrieval, you'd need to pull a larger window from `topk` and sort by salience yourself.

```scheme
;; Demonstrate: topk is recency-ranked. For salience ranking, sort the result yourself.
(begin
  ;; Write three records with different salience levels but in a known order.
  (cortex/remember \"rank-demo/low\"  \"low salience, written first\"  :salience 0.2)
  (cortex/remember \"rank-demo/high\" \"high salience, written second\" :salience 0.9)
  (cortex/remember \"rank-demo/mid\"  \"mid salience, written third\"  :salience 0.5)
  ;; topk 3 gives newest-first (mid, high, low by recency) — NOT sorted by salience.
  (let ((recent (cortex/topk 3)))
    ;; We just verify it returned 3 records; ordering depends on timestamps.
    (= (length recent) 3)))
```

Returns `#t` — three records in the window. The ordering is by timestamp (most recent first), not by salience value. If you need the highest-salience items from recent memory, call `topk` with a larger window and sort the result list by the `:salience` field of each record using `sort`.")
